<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime
       enforces library validation: every dynamic library loaded into the
       process must be signed by Apple or by this same team.

       A PyInstaller one-file binary cannot satisfy that. It carries Python's
       extension modules and their dependent dylibs inside itself, unpacks them
       to a temporary directory at startup, and dlopens them from there. Those
       files come out of upstream wheels carrying whoever built the wheel's
       signature, or none at all, so library validation rejects them and the
       binary dies before it prints anything.

       This entitlement turns that one check off. It does not disable the
       hardened runtime's other protections, and it does not weaken the
       signature on the binary itself: the binary is still Developer ID signed,
       still notarized, and still tamper-evident. It only says that the code
       this process loads is vouched for by the outer signature rather than
       checked one library at a time.

       The smoke step in the release workflow runs the signed binary before it
       is notarized, which is what proves this file is sufficient. If a future
       build dies at startup on macOS with a code signature error, this is the
       first place to look. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
